^97 The market for sound recordings had a strong, winner-take-all characteristic. Thus, increases in revenue were not shared evenly among all artists but instead flowed disproportionately to a veritable handful of superstars. As it turned out, vastly overpaying the superstars led them to work less. As a result, as industry revenue rose the top new artists each year released fewer Hot 100 hits as principal artists in the first ten years of their careers than the top new artists in low revenue periods. See id. at 157-92.

